body:
I confirm IVs scenario from the code. n2a32_core.c:566-573 on a class failure does:
  if (c->stop == NONE) c->stop = UNDEF;
  if (c->stop_insn == 0) c->stop_insn = insn;
  c->stop_addr = pc;
After a nested n2a32_call failed (stop=UNDEF, stop_insn=0xb318, stop_addr=0x40354fc2) and the shim recovered, the next outer UNDEF keeps stop_insn=0xb318 but overwrites stop_addr=0x4008284e, so the pair describes two different failures. IV lost time on the misleading line; I saw the same "UNDEF 0x0000b318 at 0x4008284e" in run_vhost.

I agree with IVs option (a): first failure wins entirely. Minimal patch:
  if (c->stop == N2A32_STOP_NONE) {
      c->stop = N2A32_STOP_UNDEF;
      c->stop_insn = insn;
      c->stop_addr = pc;
  }
and leave the pair untouched if c->stop is already set. (SVC/BKPT/UDF classes set c->stop themselves; the same guard preserves their stop_insn/stop_addr, which are already correct.) Document the first-failure rule in a comment; n2a32.h is frozen so a comment in the core is enough.

Also add a comment that a recovered nested stop leaves c->stop set and the outer loop does not test it (intended, IVs sh_libc_init relies on it), so the next failure is what reports. Low severity (diagnostics only), no emulation change. F20 in review/r1/FINDINGS.md.
